This recalibration of strategic ambiguity—maintaining uncertainty while accelerating capability provision—represented a shift from Biden-era clarity statements suggesting American intervention was likely[15][35]. The opacity regarding American commitment created uncertainty benefiting deterrence in principle, since Beijing could not assume American non-intervention, but potentially reduced Taiwan's confidence in American support, raising questions about Taiwan's defense psychology[35].

